<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
    §9.4.3's severity shapes, as path geometry.

    These are tokens rather than part of any one control's template, and they moved here from
    Themes/Generic.xaml when a second consumer appeared (#32). Generic.xaml is the control-library
    default-styles dictionary: WinUI loads it for custom control templates, but it is NOT merged
    into Application.Resources, so a page referencing a key defined only there resolves nothing at
    run time. A control template still finds these, because ThemeResource lookup walks up to the
    application.

    Each path is drawn in a 12x12 box so the five are optically consistent, and the distinctions
    are deliberately coarse - filled circle, triangle, hexagon, circled i, hollow ring - because
    they have to survive being small, being greyscale, and being seen from across a room. That is
    also what makes them the second channel A11Y-12 requires: colour convergence under protanopia
    or deuteranopia does not touch a shape, and neither does high contrast collapsing every
    severity brush onto SystemColorWindowTextColor.
-->
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <x:String x:Key="WzShapeCircle">M 0,6 A 6,6 0 1 0 12,6 A 6,6 0 1 0 0,6 Z</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="WzShapeTriangle">M 6,0 L 12,11 L 0,11 Z</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="WzShapeHexagon">M 6,0 L 11.2,3 L 11.2,9 L 6,12 L 0.8,9 L 0.8,3 Z</x:String>
    <!-- F0 is the even-odd fill rule, which is what knocks the glyph out of the disc. -->
    <x:String x:Key="WzShapeInfo">F0 M 0,6 A 6,6 0 1 0 12,6 A 6,6 0 1 0 0,6 Z M 5.1,5.1 L 6.9,5.1 L 6.9,9.4 L 5.1,9.4 Z M 5.1,2.4 L 6.9,2.4 L 6.9,4.1 L 5.1,4.1 Z</x:String>
    <x:String x:Key="WzShapeRing">F0 M 0,6 A 6,6 0 1 0 12,6 A 6,6 0 1 0 0,6 Z M 2.4,6 A 3.6,3.6 0 1 0 9.6,6 A 3.6,3.6 0 1 0 2.4,6 Z</x:String>

    <!--
        §9.9's custom icons, authored 30 Aug 2026 (#320) and retuned the same day against Tony's
        review (#345). None of them existed; the Satellites and Holdover nav items borrowed stock
        Globe and Pause, Position borrowed MapPin, the medallion borrowed a generic Warning for a
        specific state, and no sky-plot or oscillator glyph was drawn anywhere.

        DRAWN AS FILLED GEOMETRY, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME THING AS A STROKE. PathIcon FILLS its Data
        with Foreground and cannot stroke it, so every line here is authored as a filled band - a
        ring is an outer circle with an inner one knocked out, and a bar whose width equals twice
        its corner radius is a stadium. The alternative, a Path with Stroke inside a Viewbox, is not
        an IconElement and so cannot be a NavigationViewItem's Icon, which is where four of the five
        are needed.

        1.0 AND NOT §9.9'S 1.5, ACROSS ALL FIVE. The section says 1.5 px at 16 px "matches Fluent's
        optical weight so custom and stock icons in the same nav list do not read as two sets" - and
        drawn at 1.5 they did read as two sets, heavier than the Segoe Fluent glyphs beside them.
        Tony called it in review and then named the reference: the Overview glyph (#345).

        SO IT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN JUDGED. Overview's strokes render as exactly 1.0 device pixel
        at 16 px, crisply, with no antialiased spill - the font is grid-fitted at that size. 1.0 is
        that measurement. §9.9 carries the amendment, and every icon here uses it, including the two
        Tony did not name: a set whose members differ in weight is the thing the rule forbids.

        WINDING, NOT EVEN-ODD. These use F1 (nonzero) so that overlapping figures MERGE: the earth's
        equator crosses its meridian, and under the even-odd rule the crossing would cancel to a
        hole. Rings are made by drawing the inner circle with the opposite sweep flag, which is what
        turns it into a hole under nonzero.

        16x16 with a 1 px safe margin, per §9.9. Terminals and joins rounded. Monochrome, because the
        Foreground they inherit is what makes them recolour under theme and high contrast.
    -->

    <!--
        Satellite: two solar panels, a body, and the arms between them. OUTLINE ONLY (#345) - it was
        drawn solid first, which read as a much heavier object than anything around it. The lighter
        stroke is what makes the outline possible at all: at 1.5 a 3.6-wide panel leaves a 0.6 hole,
        which fills in at any scaling that is not exactly 100%; at 1.1 it leaves 1.4.

        THE BOUNDING BOX SETS THE RENDERED LINE WEIGHT, and that is the whole reason this icon is
        shaped as it is. PathIcon scales each geometry by ITS OWN bounds, so a wide, short drawing is
        magnified more than a square one: at 14 x 5.2 units the satellite rendered into a 21 x 8 px
        box and its 1.0 stroke came out at 1.45 px, against 1.14 for the stock glyphs beside it.
        Tony saw it as too heavy before the measurement found it. Squaring the bounds - arrays
        running the full height - brings the same 1.0 stroke to 1.07 px.

        A CHEAPER FIX WAS TRIED AND DOES NOT WORK: zero-area anchor subpaths at the corners
        ("M 1.5,1.5 Z M 14.5,14.5 Z") to pin the bounds while keeping a short drawing. Measured, the
        box went straight back to 21 x 8 and the stroke to 1.41 - a degenerate figure contributes
        nothing to the bounds PathIcon measures. If a future icon needs a non-square silhouette, the
        stroke has to be pre-divided by its own aspect ratio instead.

        FOUR PANELS, TWO PER WING, which is Tony's call and the right one (#345). Two tall
        rectangles reached the correct weight and read as brackets around the bus; splitting each
        wing in half is what a real array looks like and is what makes the silhouette legible at
        16 px. It costs ink over a single tall panel and buys the whole metaphor.
    -->
    <x:String x:Key="WzIconSatellite">F1 M 1.0,2.5 H 5.0 V 7.6 H 1.0 Z M 2.0,3.5 V 6.6 H 4.0 V 3.5 Z M 1.0,8.4 H 5.0 V 13.5 H 1.0 Z M 2.0,9.4 V 12.5 H 4.0 V 9.4 Z M 11.0,2.5 H 15.0 V 7.6 H 11.0 Z M 12.0,3.5 V 6.6 H 14.0 V 3.5 Z M 11.0,8.4 H 15.0 V 13.5 H 11.0 Z M 12.0,9.4 V 12.5 H 14.0 V 9.4 Z M 6.4,6.4 H 9.6 V 9.6 H 6.4 Z M 7.4,7.4 V 8.6 H 8.6 V 7.4 Z M 5.0,7.5 H 6.4 V 8.5 H 5.0 Z M 9.6,7.5 H 11.0 V 8.5 H 9.6 Z</x:String>

    <!--
        Earth: a stylized globe for §10.6, added 30 Aug 2026 at Tony's request (#345). It replaces
        stock MapPin, and it is a FIFTH custom icon where §9.9's table names four - the amendment is
        recorded there.

        A pin says "a place on a map". This page is about the receiver's own surveyed position on the
        planet, held to a metre over hours of averaging, and a globe with a meridian and an equator
        says that where a pin says the opposite - one point, chosen, provisional.
    -->
    <x:String x:Key="WzIconEarth">F1 M 1.5,8 A 6.5,6.5 0 1 0 14.5,8 A 6.5,6.5 0 1 0 1.5,8 Z M 2.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 13.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 2.5,8 Z M 5.3,8 A 2.7,5.5 0 1 0 10.7,8 A 2.7,5.5 0 1 0 5.3,8 Z M 6.3,8 A 1.7,4.5 0 1 1 9.7,8 A 1.7,4.5 0 1 1 6.3,8 Z M 2.5,7.5 H 13.5 V 8.5 H 2.5 Z</x:String>

    <!--
        Sky plot: the polar grid itself - an azimuth ring, an elevation ring, and the zenith at the
        centre, which is the drawing SkyPlotControl actually makes. Lightened and thinned out at
        Tony's review (#345): it had four concentric rings plus a dot, which at 16 px is a target
        rather than a plot.
    -->
    <x:String x:Key="WzIconSkyPlot">F1 M 1.5,8 A 6.5,6.5 0 1 0 14.5,8 A 6.5,6.5 0 1 0 1.5,8 Z M 2.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 13.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 2.5,8 Z M 4.5,8 A 3.5,3.5 0 1 0 11.5,8 A 3.5,3.5 0 1 0 4.5,8 Z M 5.5,8 A 2.5,2.5 0 1 1 10.5,8 A 2.5,2.5 0 1 1 5.5,8 Z M 7.1,8 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 0 8.9,8 A 0.9,0.9 0 1 0 7.1,8 Z</x:String>

    <!--
        Oscillator: §9.9 names the form exactly - a sine in a rounded square. The wave is two
        half-period bands rather than one path, because a band that crosses its own inflection cannot
        be written as a single offset arc pair; at 16 px the join is invisible and the shape reads.
    -->
    <x:String x:Key="WzIconOscillator">F0 M 4.5,1.5 H 11.5 A 3,3 0 0 1 14.5,4.5 V 11.5 A 3,3 0 0 1 11.5,14.5 H 4.5 A 3,3 0 0 1 1.5,11.5 V 4.5 A 3,3 0 0 1 4.5,1.5 Z M 4.5,2.5 H 11.5 A 2,2 0 0 1 13.5,4.5 V 11.5 A 2,2 0 0 1 11.5,13.5 H 4.5 A 2,2 0 0 1 2.5,11.5 V 4.5 A 2,2 0 0 1 4.5,2.5 Z M 3.5,8 A 2.5,2.5 0 0 1 8.5,8 L 7.5,8 A 1.5,1.5 0 0 0 4.5,8 Z M 7.5,8 A 2.5,2.5 0 0 0 12.5,8 L 11.5,8 A 1.5,1.5 0 0 1 8.5,8 Z</x:String>

    <!--
        Holdover: §9.9's pause inside a clock face, lightened at Tony's review (#345) while keeping
        the pause, which he confirmed reads correctly.

        This one is the reason the set was authored. The medallion had been borrowing a generic
        Warning glyph for a state that is specific, and "something is wrong" is not what holdover
        means. The oscillator is still disciplining; it is doing it from memory.
    -->
    <x:String x:Key="WzIconHoldover">F1 M 1.5,8 A 6.5,6.5 0 1 0 14.5,8 A 6.5,6.5 0 1 0 1.5,8 Z M 2.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 13.5,8 A 5.5,5.5 0 1 1 2.5,8 Z M 6.5,6.3 A 0.5,0.5 0 0 1 7.5,6.3 V 9.7 A 0.5,0.5 0 0 1 6.5,9.7 Z M 8.5,6.3 A 0.5,0.5 0 0 1 9.5,6.3 V 9.7 A 0.5,0.5 0 0 1 8.5,9.7 Z</x:String>

</ResourceDictionary>